To go boot your system with the old 2.4.xx kernel=20
you had before you can enter the following at the srm command line:

         boot diskname -flags i

instead of

         boot diskname -flags 0

You then get a aboot command line, where you can=20
navigate and for example boot the kernel that was=20
installed before the upgrade by typing "1" (profile number 1). This is

         boot diskname -flags 1

Hope that helps to start the old kernel. If the=20
mkinitrd updater did not create the links to the=20
old kernel (e.g. vmlinuz.old -> vmlinuz-2.4.27-2;=20
mkinitrd.img.old -> mkinitrd.img-2.4.27-2 inside=20
/boot) you can manually add a specific kernel by=20
entering the full boot command line (try to print=20
out profile 0 or 1 and modifiy the line with a correct kernel command line).

Uwe

